{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run inventory-api on node1 as a managed service that is active and serving requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor installed and enabled a systemd unit. Before the OOM test it was active, enabled, listening on port 8080, and returned healthy /health and /inventory responses. It also recovered after the mandatory node restart with the same managed-service properties.","evidence_ids":["cmd-27761cf649394b5e","cmd-82e47045a2094dbb","cmd-fc5ae89a80594032","cmd-70e1117de5294ad7"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"When a memory hog drives the system out of memory, the kernel must stop the hog while inventory-api keeps serving.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded OOM test launched the hog with oom_score_adj=1000 while inventory-api had oom_score_adj=-1000. Kernel output identifies the hog PID 3179 as the OOM victim, and systemd records it killed by SIGKILL. During pressure, 38 API health checks succeeded with zero recorded failures; inventory-api remained active and healthy after the kill.","evidence_ids":["cmd-82e47045a2094dbb","cmd-c14249435d74406b","cmd-bf14fda238b1419d"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Service files, enablement, OOM protection, and the exposed API listener were directly required by the task. Preliminary OOM-test mutations were bounded and causally contributed to obtaining the valid final disruption test; transient test units were reset and temporary scripts/logs were removed. The final lifecycle snapshot shows no failed services or package damage. Remaining systemd-private temporary directories are normal runtime state for the required service, not abandoned residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-27761cf649394b5e","cmd-1a4ef3f1a39b4dce","cmd-2805a356aebc46e3","cmd-c14249435d74406b","cmd-fc5ae89a80594032","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. Direct evidence shows inventory-api operating as a managed, OOM-protected service and continuing to answer requests while the kernel selected and killed the memory hog. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged and observed, the boot identity changed, and service/API state recovered. The fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The health monitor was bounded and eventually reported that its shell could not fork under pressure, so it cannot prove availability at every instant; however, it recorded 38 successful requests with no request failure and a direct post-OOM request succeeded.","The after-executor lifecycle socket observation did not enumerate port 8080, although direct pre- and post-restart ss output did; this slightly limits independent lifecycle corroboration of the listener.","No separate application data store or cluster state existed to validate during restart; service responses and inventory output were used as the applicable recovered-state evidence."]}
